          { name: "Education Quality and Professional-Class Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 41.9% college-educated adults—a substantially educated constituency—public school quality, higher education funding, and the workforce pipeline from schools to economic opportunity are material governance concerns that shape how professionals and young families evaluate candidates. This group is swing-capable: they are not locked into either coalition and will support candidates who demonstrate genuine commitment to education investment and economic mobility pathways. A legislator who engages education policy with specificity on K-12 funding adequacy, school infrastructure, and STEM workforce development is addressing the constituency most capable of moving a 54-46 race." },
          { name: "Hispanic/Latino Community as Heterogeneous Constituency", tag: "careful", why: "While 44.8% Hispanic/Latino is the dominant demographic fact, the community is economically and politically diverse: it includes affluent professionals and small business owners, working-class renters, agricultural workers, and public sector employees with different policy priorities. Borrego's 54% holds across this diversity, but any challenger or incumbent who campaigns as though Hispanic voters are a monolithic bloc will miss the real swing dynamics—which run through Hispanic professionals' views on tax policy and business growth, Hispanic renters' views on housing affordability, and Hispanic young adults' views on education and opportunity. Authentic engagement requires understanding and addressing specific economic interests within the community, not treating it as a single voting unit." },
          { name: "Treating a 54-46 Margin as a Stable Democratic Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 54-46 result in a district with 42.8% renters, substantial swing-capable educated professionals, and a median age of 37.5 is not a safe seat—it is a competitive district where any incumbent who assumes the 4-point margin will hold is underestimating the fragility of that coalition. Renters are more mobile voters than homeowners, younger adults are more persuadable than older ones, and educated professionals evaluate on specific policy outcomes rather than inherited partisan loyalty. An incumbent who consolidates the existing 54% without actively addressing what attracted the 46% and why—housing costs, economic opportunity, healthcare access—is leaving the seat vulnerable to a challenger who offers concrete answers." },
          { name: "Ignoring the 41.9% College-Educated Professional Constituency's Economic Interests", tag: "avoid", why: "A district where 41.9% of adults hold college degrees and median income is $85,486 is a constituency of professionals and knowledge workers whose support is conditional on whether government is delivering on their material interests: quality schools, business-friendly policy, healthcare access, and economic growth. Any candidate who appeals to this group on cultural messaging alone, or treats them as a captive partisan coalition, will lose swing voters to an opponent who speaks credibly to their actual economic concerns and governance expectations. The 54-46 margin reflects Borrego's success in holding this group; losing them is the most direct path to flipping the seat." }
