          { name: "Education Quality & K-12 Funding", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 49.9% college-educated adults—the largest professional and credentialed constituency in the district—education quality is both a material household concern and a signal of commitment to opportunity and social mobility. The 56-44 Democratic margin depends on college-educated voters, and these voters evaluate on whether schools are actually delivering strong outcomes, competitive teacher compensation, and modern facilities. Herndon must lead on specific K-12 funding increases, teacher retention, and measurable achievement metrics, not abstract education rhetoric, to hold this constituency against Republican school-choice messaging that will appeal to education-conscious suburban professionals." },
          { name: "Housing Affordability & Homeowner Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $85,625 median income and 64.2% homeownership, HD-28 is a district of property-owning professionals for whom mortgage costs, property taxes, and housing supply directly impact household finances. The current Democratic hold depends partly on younger renters and first-time buyers (35.8% renter rate) who benefit from affordability-focused policy, but the homeowner-majority base is persuadable if they believe Democratic governance is driving up housing costs through regulation or failing to expand housing supply. Herndon must deliver concrete policy wins on housing supply expansion, property tax fairness, and costs for homeowners—and be able to articulate these as Democratic-led solutions, not acknowledge them as areas of Republican policy superiority." },
          { name: "Hispanic Voter Persuasion & Turnout", tag: "careful", why: "The 28.4% Hispanic population is a growing constituency that delivered key support to Herndon's 56% in 2024, but Hispanic voters in suburban, property-owning, economically upwardly-mobile households are not reliably Democratic on first principles. Hispanic homeowners in HD-28 evaluate on education quality, public safety, and economic opportunity—the same metrics as white homeowners—and are vulnerable to Republican messaging on school quality, public safety, and cost-of-living if Democrats fail to deliver. Any assumption that the Hispanic constituency will turn out or support Democrats at current levels in a lower-salience cycle is strategically dangerous; Herndon must build durable relationships with Hispanic homeowners and working families through specific governance performance, not cultural appeals." },
          { name: "Taxation & Cost-of-Living Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "At $85,625 income and 64% homeownership, the district's material constituency is economically secure but fiscally attentive—and vulnerable to Republican messaging that frames Democratic governance as high-cost and tax-heavy. Republicans will message relentlessly on tax burden, regulatory cost, and government inefficiency, and this messaging has real purchase with upper-middle-income professionals who feel the actual burden of state and local taxation. Herndon cannot win a debate about whether government should be bigger or smaller; she can only win by demonstrating that Democratic governance delivers superior outcomes (better schools, safer streets, more affordable housing) for the same or lower tax burden, which requires avoiding any messaging that accepts the Republican frame that Democratic governance equals higher cost." },
          { name: "Culture-War Positioning & Identity-Based Appeals", tag: "avoid", why: "HD-28's college-educated, economically secure constituency is not primarily animated by culture-war issues; it is animated by education quality, public safety, housing costs, and whether government is competent and responsive. Any Democratic strategy that attempts to consolidate the coalition through social or cultural messaging—rather than through performance on K-12 quality, housing affordability, and public safety—concedes the terrain where suburban swing voters are actually evaluating and invites Republicans to close the margin by offering economic and governance alternatives. The 56-44 margin was won on material policy ground, not cultural alignment, and holding it requires staying on that ground." }
