        issues: [
          { name: "Working-Class Hispanic Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $38,690 median household income with 14.6% college attainment and 30.3% renters, NM-HD-32 is a working-class constituency living with real economic constraints on housing, childcare, and healthcare. Hispanic working-class voters in New Mexico respond to candidates who demonstrate specific understanding of these material concerns and offer concrete solutions — wage growth, affordable housing, healthcare cost reduction — not abstract partisan rhetoric. A representative who can point to specific legislative wins that improved economic outcomes for constituent families commands the credibility this district demands." },
          { name: "Local Constituent Service & Responsiveness", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 30.3% rents and median income at $38,690, working families in NM-HD-32 face real, urgent problems requiring immediate solutions: eviction risk, utility costs, emergency healthcare, school responsiveness. A representative who is visibly present in the district, responsive to constituent complaints, and demonstrably solving practical problems for families builds durable political support that transcends partisan margins. Conversely, a representative perceived as distant or unresponsive creates vulnerability to a challenger offering better local service." },
          { name: "Hispanic Voter Alignment as Contingent, Not Permanent", tag: "careful", why: "The 61% Republican result in a 67% Hispanic district is strong but runs counter to national Hispanic voting patterns, indicating alignment with the current incumbent's profile rather than structural partisan loyalty. Hispanic working-class voters evaluate on economic outcomes and responsive government, not inherited party affiliation; if constituent service declines or a credible challenger offers a stronger economic message, the 39% opposition is a latent base capable of growing. Any incumbent must treat this alignment as conditional on delivering for constituents, not as a permanent floor." },
          { name: "Partisan Culture-War Messaging Disconnected from Material Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "Working-class Hispanic families in NM-HD-32 with $38,690 median income are focused on economic survival — employment, housing, healthcare, schools — not abstract partisan debates. A candidate who invests campaign energy in culture-war rhetoric rather than concrete economic solutions will be perceived as out of touch with the material reality families face daily. This messaging disconnect is particularly dangerous in a 67% Hispanic district where economic concerns are primary." },
          { name: "Assuming the 61% Result Reflects Stable Base Support Rather Than Current Alignment", tag: "avoid", why: "A 61-39 result is a strong margin but not a permanent structure in a working-class district where constituent service and economic performance drive voter behavior more than inherited party loyalty. Any incumbent who campaigns as though 61% represents a safe floor — rather than a current alignment contingent on delivering for constituents — risks losing touch with the swing voters whose support determines whether the margin holds at 61% or shrinks to competitive range. The 39% opposition in a 67% Hispanic constituency is strategically significant and requires active engagement, not dismissal." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Northern New Mexico's Hispanic Working-Class District: 67% Latino, 61% Republican Margin, and a Low-Education, Low-Income Constituency Requiring Boots-on-Ground Economic Engagement",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "NM-HD-32 is a heavily Hispanic district (67.48%) with a commanding 61% Republican result — a margin that demands explanation because it runs counter to Hispanic voting patterns in most of the country and reflects a specific constituency alignment that cannot be taken for granted. The district's median household income of $38,690 is substantially below the state and national median, and college attainment at 14.6% is roughly half the national average, indicating a working-class constituency with limited educational credentials and real economic constraints. The median age of 37.3 and renter rate of 30.3% signal a mixed homeowner-renter base, but the core voter profile is a Hispanic household living on modest income, likely with children, facing direct economic pressure around housing costs, employment stability, and whether government services are functioning for their family. The 61% Republican result reflects alignment between this constituency and the incumbent — but alignment is not loyalty, and in a district where the median household is living month-to-month on $38,690, economic performance and direct constituent service are the primary levers that hold or lose this seat.",
          "The strategic vulnerability in a 61-39 result in a 67% Hispanic district is that it leaves 39% of the constituency — a Democratic base that could grow if Republican economic messaging fails to reach or persuade working-class Hispanic families. Hispanic working-class voters in New Mexico do not reflexively vote Democratic; they respond to candidates who demonstrate specific understanding of their economic lives and offer concrete solutions to the material problems they face daily. In NM-HD-32, those problems are: stable employment at wages that cover rent and childcare, healthcare access at an affordable price, whether schools are functioning adequately, and whether the local government is responsive and competent. A representative who can point to specific legislative wins — workforce development programs that led to job placements, healthcare cost reductions, or school improvements — commands the credibility this constituency demands. Conversely, a representative who abstracts away from these material concerns into partisan rhetoric, or who is perceived as unresponsive to constituent problems, creates an opening for a challenger who offers a different economic narrative. The 61% is not unshakeable; it reflects current alignment, not structural inevitability in a district this economically constrained and this predominantly working-class Hispanic."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Build economic credibility through specific constituent service: in a district where median household income is $38,690 and 14.6% have college degrees, legislative wins must translate into tangible improvements in employment, housing affordability, or healthcare cost for working families — not abstract policy positions.",
          "Engage Hispanic working-class voters on their material economic concerns, not inherited partisan assumptions: Hispanic households in NM-HD-32 evaluate on whether government is delivering for them economically, and a candidate who demonstrates specific understanding of wage stagnation, housing costs, and childcare affordability can hold or build support across the 61-39 margin.",
          "Invest in local presence and constituent responsiveness: in a district with 30.3% renters and median income at $38,690, families have real problems (eviction risk, utility costs, healthcare access) and need to see their representative solving them — boots-on-ground service in the district matters more than external visibility.",
          "Monitor Democratic challenger positioning on economic issues: the 39% opposition in a 67% Hispanic district is a latent base; if a credible challenger emerges with a focused economic message that resonates with working-class families, the 61% is contestable, particularly if incumbent constituent service is perceived as weak."
        ]
