          { name: "Renter-Focused Housing & Cost-of-Living Policy", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 21.8% renters in a district with $65,754 median household income, housing affordability is not a peripheral issue — it is a direct determinant of household economic security and family stability for more than one-fifth of the constituency. Rent increases, eviction protections, property tax policy, and utility regulation have direct material impact on daily life; a representative who cannot speak with specificity and fluency about housing policy and cost-of-living pressures for renters is not addressing a core economic concern of the district. This is the political space where both Democratic and Republican candidates can build credibility with a working-class majority." },
          { name: "Hispanic Voter Turnout & Persuasion Stability", tag: "careful", why: "A 52-48 margin in a 62.33% Hispanic district means the outcome is mathematically dependent on Hispanic voter turnout and candidate preference — any shift of 2-3 percentage points in how Hispanic voters participate or whom they support flips the seat. The incumbent's 52% suggests Hispanic consolidation was sufficient to win, but the tightness of the margin and the economic vulnerability of the constituency means this coalition is not inherently stable. Any candidate who takes the Hispanic vote for granted without active, continuous engagement on the specific economic issues that matter to working-class Hispanic families is creating an opening for a credible challenger." },
          { name: "Identity-Based Messaging Without Economic Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "In a working-class, majority-renter, 62% Hispanic district at $65,754 median household income, cultural or identity-based campaign messaging that is not anchored to concrete economic delivery on housing, wages, or childcare creates strategic vulnerability. Voters in HD-36 evaluate candidates primarily on material economic impact — whether rent is becoming more or less affordable, whether wages are rising, whether childcare is accessible — not on abstract political identity. A Democratic candidate who campaigns on identity grounds without economic specificity leaves room for a Republican opponent to contest the seat by offering credible economic messaging to the same constituency." },
          { name: "Treating a 4-Point Margin as a Durable Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 52-48 result is a plurality, not a majority, and in a district with strong demographic consolidation and measurable economic vulnerability it represents a genuinely competitive outcome, not a Democratic hold. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns this seat as though 52% represents a safe or comfortable margin is arithmetically wrong and strategically exposed. The constituency is young (38.2 median age), working-class ($65,754 income), heavily renting (21.8%), and concentrated (62% Hispanic) — all characteristics of a constituency that will shift its support if material economic conditions deteriorate or an opponent offers credible economic alternatives." }
