          { name: "Anti-Hispanic or Anti-Immigrant Positioning", tag: "avoid", why: "In a 59.15% Hispanic-plurality district, any messaging or record that appears to position Republicans as hostile to Hispanic immigration, community concerns, or economic dignity will activate the 48% opposition and depress Hispanic turnout among persuadable voters. The current 52-48 margin depends on Hispanic voter support; messaging that appears dismissive of Hispanic community perspectives or economic concerns will erode that support more rapidly than any other single factor." },
          { name: "Treating 52% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 52% result in a Hispanic-plurality, working-class, renter-significant district is not a structural Republican advantage — it is a narrow, conditional alignment that reflects either the incumbent's personal positioning or specific electoral dynamics from 2024. Any candidate who campaigns this seat as though 52% represents a comfortable hold rather than a genuinely competitive district will miss the persuadable Hispanic voters and renters whose economic concerns demand specific policy answers. The 48% opposition is substantial and organized, and the district's demographic structure makes it naturally competitive." }
