          "Engage the Hispanic community with economic specificity: at $48,198 income and 17.6% college attainment, this is a working-class constituency where wage stagnation, housing affordability, healthcare access, and whether government is actually delivering on economic opportunity are the binding issues. Absent specific constituent attention and legislative record on these concerns, the Hispanic plurality represents a latent challenger opportunity.",
          "Address aging homeowner economic security directly: at 52.4 median age with $48,198 income and 15% renter rate, the district's homeowner majority is older and income-constrained, facing rising healthcare costs, property tax pressures, and inflation-driven fixed-income squeeze. State-level policy on healthcare affordability, property tax relief, and senior services is the legislative agenda that consolidates this constituency.",
          "Do not assume the unopposed result reflects durable consolidation: in a district with a 45.88%-47.68% Hispanic-white split, a 4.87% AIAN gap, and lower-income, less-educated working-class households, the electoral foundation is genuinely competitive. The absence of a challenger in 2024 is a temporary condition, not a structural Republican advantage; the next cycle requires active engagement of both the Hispanic and aging homeowner constituencies to hold what appears safe on paper."
