          { name: "Hispanic Community Engagement and Economic Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 31.49% of the district, Hispanic constituents are the second-largest demographic group, yet the 61-39 Republican margin suggests either substantial Hispanic support for the incumbent or insufficient Hispanic voter mobilization by either party. For a challenger, building authentic engagement with Hispanic constituents around their specific economic concerns — affordable housing, healthcare access, workforce development, immigration policy impact on local labor markets — is the most direct path to narrowing or flipping the seat. For the incumbent, taking Hispanic voter support for granted in a working-class district with 37.7% renters is an active vulnerability." },
          { name: "Renter vs. Homeowner Constituency Divergence", tag: "careful", why: "With 37.7% renters and $53,601 median income, NM-HD-51 has a significant renter population whose policy priorities often diverge sharply from homeowner constituencies: renters prioritize housing affordability, eviction prevention, and access to transit and services, while homeowners prioritize property values and tax management. At 37.7%, renters are large enough to shift electoral outcomes if mobilized around their specific concerns, yet small enough that a candidate who over-indexes on homeowner issues can maintain a winning coalition. The incumbent's coalition may depend on winning both renters and homeowners by addressing their distinct concerns authentically rather than assuming alignment." },
          { name: "Treating the 61-39 Margin as a Safe Majority in a Working-Class District", tag: "avoid", why: "A 61-39 result in a district with 31.49% Hispanic population, 37.7% renter rate, and $53,601 median income is not a comfortable majority — it is a result that depends on either substantial Hispanic support for the incumbent or low Hispanic/renter voter turnout. Any candidate who campaigns as though 61% is a durable floor while neglecting the economic concerns of Hispanic constituents or renters is misreading the coalition. In a working-class district, economic anxiety can shift outcomes quickly if an alternative candidate offers credible answers." },
          { name: "Ignoring the 5.59% Demographic Gap and Potential American Indian Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "While smaller than other districts' AIAN populations, the 5.59% gap in NM-HD-51 likely represents American Indian constituents whose specific concerns — healthcare access through Indian Health Service, water rights, tribal economic development — may not be adequately addressed by either candidate. A challenger who engages authentically with this population, even at small scale, demonstrates the kind of genuine constituent attention that can shift perception and turnout. For the incumbent, overlooking this constituency entirely risks being out-positioned on issues where tribal communities have organized governance capacity." }
