          { name: "Partisan Ideology as Primary Campaign Frame", tag: "avoid", why: "In a 57-43 district with nearly equal Hispanic and white populations, $83,482 income, and 33.4% college attainment, abstract partisan positioning — conservative vs. progressive ideology, federal-state authority debates, cultural conflict narratives — will underperform relative to concrete constituent-service messaging about property taxes, healthcare, education funding, and economic opportunity. The constituency here is not motivated by deep ideological commitment to either party but by practical evaluation of which candidate better addresses their material concerns. A campaign that leads with ideology rather than kitchen-table issues concedes the persuadable middle to an opponent who frames the race around concrete governance performance." },
          { name: "Assuming Hispanic Voters Are a Campaign Target Rather Than a Core Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "At 44.09% of the district's population, Hispanic residents are not an ethnic interest group to be targeted with specialized messaging during Election Day mobilization — they are a near-majority community with mainstream economic and civic concerns that should be integrated into the core legislative agenda and constituent service portfolio. Any candidate who frames Hispanic outreach as a separate campaign element or who defaults to ethnic identity messaging rather than economic opportunity messaging will signal that Hispanic community concerns are peripheral rather than central. In a district this demographically balanced, treating Hispanic constituents as near-majority stakeholders in governance, not as a demographic target, better reflects demographic reality and builds more authentic political alignment." }
