          "Engage the Hispanic community (33.1%) with specific economic and healthcare messaging rather than abstract cultural appeals: wage and hour enforcement in service and agricultural sectors, healthcare access including reproductive healthcare, and small business support are the material issues that anchor Hispanic constituent relationships in working-class New Mexico districts.",
          "Address the 69.1% without bachelor's degrees through workforce development and wage policy specificity: apprenticeships, credential programs, and enforcement of prevailing wage standards in state contracting are the mechanisms through which working-class adults without four-year degrees build economic mobility — and they are more credible than post-hoc education messaging.",
          "Recognize that unopposed incumbency in 2024 masks structural vulnerability, not strength: a credible challenger with a message addressing housing, healthcare, and wage concerns targeting the young, renter-heavy, working-class plurality could plausibly compete in this district — do not mistake the absence of opposition for the strength of the existing coalition."
