          { name: "Educational Attainment & Economic Mobility Pipeline", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 15.6% college attainment rate is substantially below state average and signals a constituency where most adults and families lack post-secondary credentials — and where access to affordable, geographically accessible post-secondary education is a primary pathway to economic mobility. In a district with 33.8-year median age, this is a young population where educational investment decisions (K-12 funding, community college affordability, credential program alignment with regional employment) have direct bearing on whether the next generation achieves greater economic security than their parents. An incumbent who engages education funding and workforce development specifically rather than generically creates a durable constituency; one who does not creates vulnerability to a challenger who will." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Specificity & Language-Accessible Services", tag: "careful", why: "At 78.01% Hispanic with a median age of 33.8, NM-SD-14 is a majority-Hispanic district where Hispanic constituent concerns are not a subset of the district's agenda — they are the core of the district's agenda. Healthcare access, education, and labor standards as they affect the Hispanic community are not special interests to be managed; they are the material reality of three-quarters of the constituents. An incumbent who treats Hispanic community concerns with specific, fluent engagement builds real political strength; one who relies on generic minority-community framing or treats Hispanic constituent priorities as subordinate to broader state narrative risks activating latent opposition among a constituency that has the capacity to organize alternative representation if it concludes the incumbent does not prioritize its economic and social concerns." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Victory as Evidence of a Secure Seat", tag: "avoid", why: "The 2024 unopposed result is a snapshot of Republican resource allocation and strategic choice at one moment in time, not evidence that NM-SD-14 is structurally Democratic or immune to competitive challenge. In a purple state with a young, working-class, majority-Hispanic constituency where economic anxiety is evident in the median income and college attainment data, an incumbent who treats the unopposed 2024 result as permission to govern at a distance from constituent economic concerns has created the conditions for a credible challenger in the next cycle. Any incumbent messaging that relies on the 2024 result as proof of strength rather than on demonstrated constituent service and economic policy wins is strategically vulnerable." },
          { name: "Neglecting the Renter Constituency (18.3%) in Housing Policy", tag: "avoid", why: "While 81.7% homeownership might suggest a homeowner-dominant constituency, the 18.3% renter rate in a young district with median age 33.8 represents a specific, politically significant cohort — young families, service-sector workers, and lower-income households without property ownership who face direct rental market pressure and affordability constraints. An incumbent who frames housing policy exclusively around property owner concerns (property taxes, residential stability) while neglecting renter-specific issues (rent stabilization, renter protections, affordable multifamily development) risks activating opposition among a constituency that is disproportionately young and Hispanic and capable of organizing alternative representation if it concludes the incumbent is not serving its economic interests." }
