          { name: "Working-Family Economic Security Across Pluralistic Base", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 41.93% white, 39.42% Hispanic, and ~12.65% AIAN demographic composition, combined with 35.4% college education and 39.8 median age, describes a pluralistic working-class constituency where partisan identity is secondary to whether government delivers on childcare affordability, healthcare access, and wage growth. Berghmans' 61% indicates that voters across this base perceive her as advancing these material concerns; any candidate defending or challenging this seat must translate partisan positioning into specific economic policy that resonates across all three communities simultaneously, not campaign to them separately." },
          { name: "AIAN Community Engagement & Turnout", tag: "careful", why: "The ~12.65% demographic gap represents Native American constituents whose turnout and mobilization patterns can shift a district this close to competitive margins; tribal healthcare access, economic development priorities, and state-Indian affairs are the activating issues for this community. A Democrat who neglects AIAN mobilization risks suppressed turnout; a Republican who engages this community authentically on issues that matter to tribal constituencies can shift the overall result by 3-4 points. Active outreach is not optional in a 61-39 seat where the margin is held by cross-community coalition rather than inherited partisan advantage." },
          { name: "Treating 61% as a Durable Incumbent Advantage", tag: "avoid", why: "A 61% result in a working-class district where housing and economic concerns drive voting behavior is not a durable floor — it is a margin held by perceived delivery on material constituency concerns. If Berghmans faces a challenger who credibly argues she has not delivered measurably on housing affordability or healthcare costs while offering specific alternatives, the margin can compress substantially. This is a seat where partisan baseline is secondary to constituent perception of incumbent performance." },
          { name: "Assuming White Homeowner Stability in a Renter-Majority District", tag: "avoid", why: "Although 41.93% white, NM-SD-15 is 53.1% renters — meaning the white constituency is not uniformly homeowner-advantaged and contains significant numbers of renters experiencing the same housing cost burden as their Hispanic and AIAN neighbors. Any campaign strategy that assumes the white vote is a stable Republican-leaning base without addressing renters' economic concerns within that community will misread the actual composition and vulnerability of the district. Working-class white voters in a high-housing-cost environment are not automatically conservative if they perceive that alternative candidates address their material concerns more effectively." }
