          "Consolidate across the pluralistic base by addressing working-family economic security with specificity: childcare costs, healthcare access, and wage growth are the shared concerns of the white 41.93%, Hispanic 39.42%, and AIAN ~12.65% constituencies that produced Berghmans' 61%; generic partisan messaging will not hold a cross-community coalition built on material delivery.",
          "Understand the 39% opposition in the context of the district's working-class profile: the Republican vote is likely concentrated among the 41.93% white, homeowner-plurality share and some portion of Hispanic conservatives; a challenger who can argue the incumbent has failed on housing affordability or healthcare access while offering credible alternatives can peel votes from this coalition.",
          "Treat the AIAN ~12.65% gap as a turnout and mobilization priority: tribal healthcare access, economic development, and state-Indian affairs are the issues that activate this community; a Democrat who neglects it risks suppressed turnout, while a Republican who engages it authentically can shift the seat by 3-4 points in a district this close to the margin."
