          "The district's median age of 40.5 places it in a sweet spot for healthcare messaging—old enough to care about access and affordability, young enough that younger family members remain a planning priority. Education will remain a perennial winner because the college-educated plurality has proven willing to support public spending on schools, and working-age parents across all backgrounds view education as the primary vehicle for upward mobility. The one tactical landmine is water rights and environmental regulation: the North Central geography makes water economics real, and while affluent voters may embrace conservation, Hispanic/Latino working families and agricultural interests may prioritize access and use rights. Candidates must separate conservation stewardship from economic punitivism."
