          "The 22% renter rate in a district with $118K median income suggests a bifurcated constituency: homeowners with substantial equity and financial security alongside educated renters (often younger professionals, service workers, and early-career employees) whose housing costs consume a larger share of income despite above-median earnings. This split creates distinct economic priorities: homeowners focus on property taxes, school funding, and neighborhood stability; renters prioritize housing affordability, transit, and economic mobility. At 60% college-educated and 10% Asian, the district likely includes professional workers, tech employees, healthcare professionals, and academics whose policy concerns center on education quality, healthcare access, skilled-workforce development, and tax efficiency — not cultural grievance or partisan theater. A candidate who wins here must address the material economic concerns of both constituencies simultaneously and demonstrate genuine competence on the specific issues this educated, diverse, high-income electorate evaluates on."
