          "NM-SD-28 presents one of the most analytically revealing partisan-demographic misalignments in this district set: a 55% Republican victory in a district that is 58.15% Hispanic and 37.17% white. This result is not explained by Hispanic Republican voting alone—it requires understanding a specific Anglo homeowner coalition (the 70.5% owner-occupied housing base, median age 42.7, median income $42,663) that is holding the seat despite demographic composition that should favor Democratic performance. Ramos's 55% win likely reflects both consolidation of the white homeowner plurality and meaningful Hispanic crossover support, driven either by personal candidate strength, specific local governance issues, or cultural conservatism among older Hispanic homeowners and business owners in the district. The strategic fact is that this 55% margin is constructed on two constituencies—Anglo homeowners and Spanish-surname Republican voters—whose relative size and cohesion will determine whether this seat becomes more or less competitive in subsequent cycles. Any challenger must understand which of these two groups is the actual margin-holder and target accordingly; any incumbent must understand that 45% opposition in a 58% Hispanic district is not a stable equilibrium.",
