          "Engage the Hispanic/Latino plurality (49.68%) with material, specific policy positions on wages, employment pathways, and economic mobility — not token acknowledgment; this community's voting behavior is organized around concrete economic outcomes, and positioning on vocational training, small business access, and wage standards speaks to their lived concerns more effectively than generic partisan messaging.",
          "Address the homeowner-renter economic divide explicitly: property tax management and stability matter to the 78.6% homeowner majority, while housing access and cost matter to renters; a legislator who can articulate how their record serves both constituencies simultaneously builds broader economic coalition rather than defaulting to the homeowner majority.",
