          { name: "Rural & Agricultural Economy Viability", tag: "lean-into", why: "Southern New Mexico's agricultural economy — centered on cotton, pecans, and chile production in the Rio Grande Valley — faces ongoing pressure from water scarcity, climate stress, and commodity price volatility. The district's 34.1-year median age and working-class income profile reflect families whose economic stakes are tied directly to farming and ranching viability. A legislator who engages water policy, agricultural infrastructure, and the state-federal relationship on rural economic development with concrete specificity demonstrates that they understand the material foundation of the district's economy rather than treating agriculture as heritage rather than livelihood." },
          { name: "White Community Economic Concerns & Political Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "At 26.93%, the white community in NM-SD-36 represents approximately one-quarter of the district and accounts for the 44% Republican opposition to Steinborn's 56% win. This is a significant constituency whose economic concerns — particularly in rural and exurban parts of the district — warrant specific engagement rather than assumption that the white vote is monolithically Republican. The 34.1-year median age and $49,942 income suggest white households in this district face similar economic pressures as the Hispanic majority, and the partisan division may reflect perception gaps about who is addressing those concerns rather than fundamental ideological disagreement." },
          { name: "Treating the 44% Opposition as a Base to Ignore", tag: "avoid", why: "A 56-44 margin in a heavily Hispanic, working-class district is a solid Democratic win but not a landslide — and treating the Republican opposition as a fixed base with no swing potential mistakes the strategic reality of the district. In a constituency where median income is under $50,000 and economic security is a direct concern for both Hispanic and white households, economic messaging that resonates across ethnic lines is more durable than partisan positioning that assumes the 56% coalition is locked in." },
          { name: "Positioning as a Representative of 'Hispanic Interests' Rather Than District Interests", tag: "avoid", why: "While Hispanic community engagement is a core strategic imperative in a 67.55% Hispanic district, framing the representative's role primarily through an ethnic-identity lens rather than as an advocate for all working-class constituents' economic security is both analytically incomplete and electorally risky. The 36% renter rate, $49,942 income, and 22.8% college attainment describe a constituency defined by economic class and housing insecurity more fundamentally than by ethnicity. A representative who addresses economic security and workforce development for all working families — and happens to represent a majority-Hispanic district — builds a more durable coalition than one who positions themselves primarily as an ethnic advocate." }
